<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Tosa Province</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Tosa Province was an old province of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> on the southern, Pacific coast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shikoku">Shikoku</a>, corresponding to modern Kōchi Prefecture. Ruled by the Chōsokabe clan, who unified it by 1575, it passed after 1600 to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yamauchi-kazutoyo">Yamauchi Kazutoyo</a> as the Tosa domain. Its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samurai">samurai</a>, among them Sakamoto Ryōma, played a major part in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/meiji-restoration">Meiji Restoration</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/tosa-province.jpg" alt="Tosa Province" width="320" height="335" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Ash_Crow · CC BY 2.5 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Provinces_of_Japan-Tosa.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Tosa Province lay in the south of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shikoku">Shikoku</a>, bordering Awa to the northeast and Iyo to the northwest. Its abbreviated name was Doshū. In the Gokishichidō system it belonged to the Nankaidō circuit, and under the Engishiki classification it ranked as a middle country in importance and a far country in distance from the capital. The provincial capital stood in what is now the city of Nankoku, and the ichinomiya, the province's chief shrine, is the Tosa shrine in the city of Kōchi.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The Ritsuryo reforms formed the province by combining the territories of the Tosa kuni no miyatsuko in the east and the Hata kuni no miyatsuko in the west. The name appears in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nihon-shoki">Nihon Shoki</a> in an entry dated March 675, usually in connection with natural disasters, among them the Hakuhō earthquake of 684, in which a tsunami reportedly swept away a ship carrying the provincial governor. From the Asuka period the province seems to have served as a place of exile. At the end of the Heian period the Heike clan exiled there Minamoto no Mareyoshi, younger brother of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/minamoto-no-yoritomo">Minamoto no Yoritomo</a>, and other exiles included Fujiwara no Moronaga, Emperor Tsuchimikado and Prince Takanaga.</p><p>In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kamakura">Kamakura</a> period the Ichijō family of court nobles established a huge shōen estate in western Tosa and held it into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sengoku-period">Sengoku period</a>. In the Muromachi period the Hosokawa clan were shugo but preferred to rule through the Ohira clan from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyoto">Kyoto</a>. When income from the estate faltered because of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/onin-war">Onin War</a>, Kanpaku Ichijo Norifusa moved to Tosa with the help of the Ohira and became a local power in the west. The rest of the province was held by clans including the Motoyama, Aki, Kira, Tsuno, Chōsokabe and Kosokabe. Chōsokabe Motochika brought all of Tosa under the Chōsokabe and went on to expand across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shikoku">Shikoku</a>, until <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toyotomi-hideyoshi">Toyotomi Hideyoshi</a> confined him to Tosa.</p><p>His son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chosokabe-morichika">Chōsokabe Morichika</a> was dispossessed for siding with the losing Western army at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-sekigahara">Sekigahara</a> in 1600, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokugawa-shogunate">Tokugawa shogunate</a> gave the province to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yamauchi-kazutoyo">Yamauchi Kazutoyo</a>, whose clan ruled it as daimyō of the Tosa Domain until the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/meiji-restoration">Meiji Restoration</a>. Under the Yamauchi, Kōchi Castle was built and Kōchi became the capital. In the Bakumatsu period Sakamoto Ryōma, Nakaoka Shintarō, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/itagaki-taisuke">Itagaki Taisuke</a> and Gotō Shōjirō, among others, were active in overthrowing the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shogun">shogunate</a> and founding the early Meiji government.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>An early Meiji government survey recorded 348 villages in the province, with a total kokudaka of 494,087 koku. After the abolition of the han system in 1871 Tosa Province became Kōchi Prefecture. The Imperial Japanese Navy battleship Tosa, lead ship of its class, was named after the province.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Tosa Province” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Chosokabe were one of seven local warrior families of <b>Tosa</b>, based at Okoh castle.</blockquote><a href="/state/chosokabe-clan-domain">Chosokabe clan domain</a></li><li><blockquote>After Chosokabe Morichika lost <b>Tosa</b> for joining the western army at Sekigahara, Tokugawa Ieyasu gave the province to Yamauchi Kazutoyo, who arrived in 1601 and built Kochi castle.</blockquote><a href="/state/tosa-domain">Tosa Domain</a></li></ul></section>
